cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Tallystick: an adversarial harness for accountability in delegated agentic action"
message: "If you use this corpus or harness, please cite it as below."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Koshy
    given-names: Nitin Stephen
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0009-2690-0588"
abstract: >-
  A cryptography-free adversarial scenario corpus and adjudication harness for
  studying accountability in delegation disputes involving AI agents. The
  two-party corpus has 850 scenarios across 34 adversarial classes and 18
  adjudication baselines, with a derived boundary on what any
  evidence-determined mechanism can resolve: where two worlds produce identical
  evidence, differ in whether any account diverges from the record, and someone
  is dishonest in the world where one does, no mechanism resolves both, and the
  cost can be denominated but not removed. Includes a documented record of six
  hardening declaration defects, later publication and reporting drifts, and the
  build stages written to catch those failure modes. Version 0.11 adds a
  three-party phase (principal, intermediary, executor): declarative
  interaction-protocol specifications, a separate corpus, and a generalisation
  of the boundary from equal to intersecting divergence sets, with localisation
  shown to require equality. The two rules agree only while each world has at
  most one diverging party.
keywords:
  - accountability
  - delegation
  - AI agents
  - non-repudiation
  - fair exchange
  - adversarial evaluation
  - multi-agent systems
  - interaction protocols
